# 8. The Zero-Frequency Problem 🚨

Suppose the word:

```text
"Bitcoin"
```

never appeared in the training data.

Then:

\[
P(Bitcoin|Spam)=0
\]

Since Naive Bayes multiplies probabilities:

\[
P(C)
\times
P(X_1|C)
\times
0
=0
\]

The entire probability becomes zero.

This is a serious problem.

---

# 9. Laplace Smoothing ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

To solve the zero-frequency problem, we use **Laplace Smoothing**.

Basic idea:

> Add a small value to observed counts so unseen events do not receive a probability of exactly zero.

A common form is:

\[
\boxed{
P(word|class)
=
\frac{Count(word,class)+1}
{TotalWords+VocabularySize}
}
\]

This is often called:

> **Add-one smoothing**

---

# Example

Without smoothing:

```text
Bitcoin Count = 0
Probability = 0
```

With smoothing:

```text
Bitcoin Count = 0 + 1
Probability > 0
```

---

# 10. Log Probabilities ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Suppose we multiply:

\[
0.001
\times
0.002
\times
0.0001
\]

The result becomes extremely small.

With many features, this can cause:

> **Numerical underflow**

Instead of multiplying probabilities, we take logarithms.

Because:

\[
\log(a\times b)
=
\log(a)+\log(b)
\]

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\log P(C|X)
\propto
\log P(C)
+
\sum_i\log P(X_i|C)
}
\]

This is much more numerically stable.

---

# 11. Edge Cases & Failure Modes 🚨

## ❌ Features Are Strongly Dependent

Naive Bayes assumes conditional independence.

Example:

```text
Rain = Yes
Umbrella = Yes
```

These features may be strongly related.

The independence assumption may oversimplify reality.

---

## ❌ Zero Probabilities

Solved using:

> Laplace Smoothing

---

## ❌ Poor Probability Calibration

Naive Bayes can classify well while producing probability estimates that may not always be perfectly calibrated.

So:

```text
0.90 probability
```

does not automatically mean the model is perfectly calibrated in a real-world deployment.

---

## ❌ Continuous Data Distribution Mismatch

Gaussian Naive Bayes assumes a Gaussian-like distribution for continuous features within each class.

If this assumption is very poor, performance can suffer.

