# 22. Explain to an Interviewer — 60-Second Script 🎤

> Naive Bayes is a supervised probabilistic classification algorithm based on Bayes' theorem. It predicts the class with the highest posterior probability by combining the prior probability of a class with the likelihood of observing the input features. The algorithm is called naive because it assumes that features are conditionally independent given the class. Common variants include Gaussian Naive Bayes for continuous features, Multinomial Naive Bayes for count-based features such as word frequencies, and Bernoulli Naive Bayes for binary features. It is computationally efficient and works particularly well for high-dimensional text classification problems such as spam detection and sentiment analysis.

